    "question": "Don buys recyclable bottles in a small town. Shop A normally sells him 150 bottles, shop B sells him 180 bottles and Shop C sells him the rest. How many bottles does Don buy from Shop C if he is capable of buying only 550 bottles?",
    "gold": "220",
    "answer": "Between Shop A and Shop B he buys 150 bottles+180 bottles=<<150+180=330>>330 bottles\nThus he can buy 550 bottles-330 bottles=<<550-330=220>>220 bottles from shop C\n#### 220"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many bottles Don buys from Shop C, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. Identify the total number of bottles Don is capable of buying.\n2. Determine how many bottles are sold by Shop A and Shop B.\n3. Subtract the number of bottles sold by Shop A and Shop B from the total number of bottles to find out how many bottles are sold by Shop C.\n\nLet's start with the total number of bottles Don is capable of buying:\n\\[ \\text{Total bottles} = 550 \\]\n\nNext, we know the number of bottles sold by Shop A and Shop B:\n\\[ \\text{Bottles from Shop A} = 150 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Bottles from Shop B} = 180 \\]\n\nNow, we calculate the total number of bottles sold by Shop A and Shop B:\n\\[ \\text{Total bottles from Shop A and Shop B} = 150 + 180 = 330 \\]\n\nTo find out how many bottles are sold by Shop C, we subtract the number of bottles sold by Shop A and Shop B from the total number of bottles:\n\\[ \\text{Bottles from Shop C} = 550 - 330 = 220 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of bottles Don buys from Shop C is:\n\\[ \\boxed{220} \\]",
